{
  "page": 64,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "332",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        39.52751922607422,
        57.665863037109375,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        78.57550048828125,
        39.52751922607422,
        316.3913879394531,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "fallacious minor mode which in a marginal posterior is stronger than the main mode of the full posterior.",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        57.378700256347656,
        438.23394775390625,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thus, it might be interesting to test whether PMC, instead of SMC, methods could be combined with",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        67.3412857055664,
        438.2330017089844,
        75.65313720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "MCMC methods in a fashion suggested by the authors, and whether it would improve the performance",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        77.30387115478516,
        438.2222900390625,
        85.61572265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in these kinds of problem.",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        87.2664566040039,
        141.12513732910156,
        95.57830810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Jonghyun Yun and Yuguo Chen (University of Illinois at Urbana—Champaign)",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        107.19156646728516,
        335.4494323730469,
        115.50341796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We congratulate the authors on successfully combining two popular sampling tools, sequential Monte",
      "bbox": [
        42.709930419921875,
        117.1541519165039,
        438.226806640625,
        125.46600341796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Carlo (SMC) and Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods. We discuss two speciﬁc implementation",
      "bbox": [
        42.709930419921875,
        127.11673736572266,
        438.2276611328125,
        135.4285888671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "issues of particle MCMC (PMCMC) algorithms.",
      "bbox": [
        42.709930419921875,
        137.07933044433594,
        227.6329803466797,
        145.39117431640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In PMCMC sampling, proposing a single sample at each iteration requires N particles. That means",
      "bbox": [
        51.67633056640625,
        147.0419158935547,
        438.2240905761719,
        155.353759765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "running PMCMC algorithms for L iterations needs NL particles. If we can afford to generate only a ﬁxed",
      "bbox": [
        42.709930419921875,
        157.00450134277344,
        438.2261047363281,
        165.31634521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "number NÅ of particles, a practical question is how to balance between N and L under the constraint",
      "bbox": [
        42.71083068847656,
        163.71226501464844,
        438.22412109375,
        175.2789306640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that NL=NÅ. We did a simulation study on model (14)–(15) with known parameters σ2",
      "bbox": [
        42.7108154296875,
        173.97970581054688,
        372.23028564453125,
        185.24151611328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "V =10 and σ2",
      "bbox": [
        368.9198913574219,
        175.81549072265625,
        419.0152893066406,
        187.55551147460938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "W =1.",
      "bbox": [
        415.70489501953125,
        176.17715454101562,
        438.2215881347656,
        187.55551147460938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Let NÅ =1000000. We simulated 100 sequences of observations y1:300 from the model. For each sequence,",
      "bbox": [
        42.70916748046875,
        182.88491821289062,
        438.22186279296875,
        196.5496826171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "four particle independent Metropolis–Hastings (PIMH) samplers with different combinations of N and",
      "bbox": [
        42.709442138671875,
        196.8555145263672,
        438.22186279296875,
        205.1673583984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "L were applied to estimate the states x1:300. The standard SMC method in Section 2.2.1 with 1000000",
      "bbox": [
        42.70947265625,
        206.8180694580078,
        438.2274169921875,
        215.84437561035156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "particles is also included in the comparison. The performance criterion is the root-mean-squared error",
      "bbox": [
        42.70957946777344,
        216.7808074951172,
        438.2289733886719,
        225.0926513671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "RMSE between the true xi and the estimates:",
      "bbox": [
        42.70957946777344,
        226.7433624267578,
        213.5347442626953,
        236.40029907226562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
